data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

The EF Core Cosmos DB provider **removed the sync-over-async opt-in entirely**. In .NET 10, you could set `EnableSyncOverAsync` to keep synchronous LINQ working — that escape hatch is gone.

Every synchronous call in your repository — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()` — will now throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime because the underlying Cosmos SDK is async-only.

## The fix

Convert to async throughout:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- The async LINQ methods come from `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` (the `using` you likely already have).
- `Add()` is an in-memory operation and stays synchronous — only the I/O call (`SaveChangesAsync`) needs to change.
- This will ripple up the call chain: callers must also become `async`/`await` all the way to the controller or endpoint.
